  "issues": [
    "MISSING CRITICAL BASELINE — 'Behavioral Constraint Prompt' (BCP): Seshadri et al. (the primary source paper) independently demonstrates in their 'minimal intervention' section that a targeted behavioral constraint prompt — specifically limiting politeness use to 'at most one or two times across the entire conversation' — causes 11 of 18 tasks to shift difficulty bins and drops overall simulated success rate from 50.0% to 46.7%. This is a direct behavioral-rate-targeting system prompt that already partially achieves DemoCalib's stated goal. None of DemoCalib's five baselines implements this approach: Baseline (a) is a generic demographic persona prompt (HUMAINE-style), not a behavioral rate-targeted prompt. Without a 'Baseline (f): BCP — simulator prompted to ask questions in ~10% of turns and use politeness markers in ~20% of turns' (a zero-compute prompting baseline), DemoCalib cannot credibly claim DPO fine-tuning adds value over simply encoding the target rates in the system prompt. Reviewers familiar with Seshadri et al. will raise this immediately. The fix is low-cost: BCP requires no training, just running the evaluation with a rate-targeting system prompt.",
    "MISSED DOWNSTREAM ANCHOR FOR INDIAN ENGLISH GROUP: Seshadri et al. Table 3 reports India-based participants achieved approximately 46.2% agent success rate (versus ~50% for SAE). DemoCalib's proxy downstream evaluation only uses the AAVE anchor (~39%) for directional validation. Including the Indian English group's success rate anchor (~46%) would strengthen the proxy evaluation to cover both calibration groups — and this data exists in the same cited paper. This is a minor omission but represents a missed opportunity to make the Indian English arm's downstream evaluation as concrete as the AAVE arm's."
  ],
  "feedback": "DemoCalib has resolved all issues from Rounds 1–7 comprehensively. The AFAA workshop name is confirmed correct (Algorithmic Fairness Across Alignment Procedures and Agentic Systems, oral status verified via OpenReview). All behavioral rates, affiliations, and citation statuses are factually accurate. No new competing paper was found in April–May 2026 that closes the specific gap. One critical fix and one minor enhancement remain:\n\n**Priority 1 — Add Behavioral Constraint Prompt (BCP) as Baseline (f):** Seshadri et al.'s own 'minimal intervention' section documents that prompting the simulator to limit politeness to 'at most one or two times' shifts 11/18 tasks' difficulty bins and changes aggregate success rates materially. This is a zero-compute prompting baseline that directly targets behavioral rates — and it is absent from DemoCalib's five-baseline comparison. Add: 'Baseline (f): BCP — Qwen 2.5 7B-Instruct prompted with explicit per-turn behavioral rate targets: ask questions in approximately 10% of turns, use politeness markers in approximately 20% of turns, derived directly from Seshadri et al. Table 6 calibration targets.' This baseline tests whether DemoCalib's DPO fine-tuning is necessary: if BCP achieves comparable behavioral rate matching without training, the DPO contribution is incremental. If DPO outperforms BCP on held-out test traces and/or proxy downstream evaluation, the paper has a crisp demonstration of added value. Either outcome is publishable — the comparison is informative regardless of direction. Given that behavioral constraint prompting is trivially implementable (no gradient updates, no GPU cost), its absence will be noticed by any reviewer who has read Seshadri et al. closely.\n\n**Priority 2 (minor) — Include Indian English success rate anchor in proxy evaluation:** Add one sentence: 'For the Indian English group, we additionally compare calibrated simulator success rate estimates to Seshadri et al.'s published India-based participant success rate of approximately 46.2%, providing a downstream validation anchor for both calibration groups.' This costs nothing (the data is in the already-cited paper) and makes the Indian English arm's downstream evaluation as concrete as the AAVE arm's.\n\nWith these two additions — one critical experiment (BCP baseline, zero GPU cost) and one sentence (Indian English anchor) — the paper is ready for EMNLP 2026 Findings submission.",
  "reason": "All Round 7 issues resolved and all factual claims verified; one critical missing baseline (behavioral constraint prompt from Seshadri et al.'s own 'minimal intervention') prevents DemoCalib from demonstrating DPO superiority over the simplest possible alternative."
}
